# Scoping Renovate to Specific Packages

This repo pins its Java dependencies (`apps/java/pom.xml`) against the
private Lightwell registry as their primary index/repository, which only
hosts a subset of Maven packages (currently `org.json:json`). Renovate
treats that primary registry as authoritative for every package in the
manifest, so any package Lightwell doesn't remediate gets a 404 and fails
lookup, even with a fallback registry configured.

To keep Renovate scoped to only the packages Lightwell actually remediates,
disable the manager by default and re-enable it only for the specific
packages, with `matchFileNames` + `matchPackageNames`. Renovate applies
`packageRules` in order and later matching rules override earlier ones, so
list the manager's disable rule before its enable rule:

```json
"packageRules": [
  {
    "matchManagers": ["maven"],
    "enabled": false
  },
  {
    "matchManagers": ["maven"],
    "matchFileNames": ["apps/java/**"],
    "matchDatasources": ["maven"],
    "matchPackageNames": ["org.json:json"],
    "enabled": true,
    "registryUrls": [
      "https://packages.redhat.com/lightwell/java/remediated",
      "https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2/"
    ]
  }
]
```

Do not "fix" 404s by adding a catch-all `registryUrls` fallback to the
public registry for every package -- that reintroduces update PRs for
packages that are out of scope for the Lightwell remediation workflow.
When adding a new package to the Lightwell registry, add its name to
`matchPackageNames` in the enable rule rather than relaxing the
default-disabled rule.

`additionalBranchPrefix: "{{parentDir}}-"` (already set at the top level)
splits Renovate PRs by the package file's parent directory.
